{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and City Lights: My Japan Adventure**\n\nLanding in Tokyo felt like stepping into a living postcard. The neon glow of Shibuya Crossing greeted me at night, a sea of umbrellas and laughter weaving through the scramble. By morning, I traded the bustle for serenity at Meiji Shrine, where towering cedars whispered ancient prayers and the scent of incense drifted on the breeze.\n\nA bullet train whisked me to Kyoto in just over two hours. The city’s temples unfolded like pages of a history book. I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless torii gates, each vermilion arch framing a new vista of forested hills. In the afternoon, I sat beside a koi pond at Kinkaku‑ji, watching the Golden Pavilion shimmer on the water’s surface—a perfect moment of stillness amid the travel rush.\n\nNo trip to Japan is complete without indulging in its cuisine. I slurped steaming bowls of ramen in a tiny alleyway shop, savored melt‑in‑your‑mouth sushi at Tsukiji’s outer market, and treated myself to matcha‑flavored wagashi while overlooking a traditional tea ceremony in Gion. The flavors were as varied as the landscapes, each bite telling a story of craftsmanship and season.\n\nEvenings were spent wandering through Osaka’s Dotonbori district, where giant crab signs blinked above bustling stalls serving takoyaki and okonomiyaki. The city’s energy was infectious, and I found myself joining locals in a spontaneous karaoke session that lasted well past midnight.\n\nAs my journey ended, I carried home more than souvenirs—I carried the quiet reverence of a shrine, the pulse of neon streets, and the warmth of strangers who shared a smile over a bowl of noodles. Japan reminded me that adventure lives both in the grand sights and the simple, everyday moments. Until next time, さようなら.", "logprobs": [{"token": "We", "logprob": -0.17234905064105988}, {"token": " need